# RP-5.2: Verifiable Web of Code

## Abstract
Traditional package managers focus on discovery and speed. This focus creates significant security vulnerabilities. This paper presents the architecture of a verifiable web of code for the openOODA ecosystem. The design proposes a decentralized package manager. In this system, imported modules have mathematical proofs. Packages must include a strict capability manifest. This system allows developers and artificial intelligence agents to import code with mathematical trust. The architecture prevents malicious installation scripts. It stops capability leakage. It prevents unauthorized system access.

## Introduction
The openOODA project uses artificial intelligence to generate code. It also uses code from multiple external parties. Traditional package managers rely on reputation. They use optional signatures. They fix problems after the problems occur. These older systems fail to stop typosquatting. They fail to stop compromised maintainer accounts. They fail to stop malicious installation scripts. Signatures prove the identity of the author. They do not prove the safety of the code.

In the openOODA ecosystem, artificial intelligence agents import code very quickly. Humans cannot review the code at this speed. Therefore, the system must enforce strict rules. Packages must declare their capabilities explicitly. For example, a network library must request a network capability in its application programming interface. If it does not request this capability, the system considers the package invalid. Furthermore, packages must include mathematical contracts. These contracts form the proof surface of the language. This paper explains the theoretical design of zero-trust packages. It details the design of a verifiable web of code.

## Related Work
Earlier research explored the concept of proof-carrying code. Necula and Lee showed that a host system can safely run untrusted code if the code contains a formal proof of its safety. Modern systems like The Update Framework secure the software supply chain. They use cryptographic signatures and transparency logs. Miller established the principles of robust composition. His work showed how capability-based systems can control access securely. The verifiable web of code combines these ideas. It applies them to a decentralized package registry for artificial intelligence agents.

## Architecture and Methodology
The architecture of the verifiable web of code uses a zero-trust import pipeline. The pipeline fetches a package using secure distribution frameworks. First, the system verifies the cryptographic signature of the package. Second, the system verifies the inclusion of the package in a transparency log. Third, the system checks the capability manifest of the package. It compares this manifest against the granted authority of the importing module. Finally, the system runs the contract verifications and the proof obligations. The system completes all these steps before it links the package into the build.

### Capability Manifests
Capability manifests are a primary component of this design. The system does not permit general imports. The declared capabilities of the package must match the static analysis of the package body. A module cannot use false manifests to gain system authority. If a module requires access to the file system, it must declare this requirement explicitly. The system uses a fail-closed design. The import fails immediately if the using module cannot provide the necessary capabilities to the imported module.

### Decentralized Registry Nodes
The design supports decentralized registry nodes. These nodes store package manifests and mathematical proofs. The system uses transparency logs to mint packages cryptographically. The system does not require a blockchain. This design isolates global fuzzing networks from the package registry. Fuzz nodes trade inputs and test cases. They do not trade authority. The registry nodes do not execute package code at index time. The packages do not contain installation scripts. This rule prevents arbitrary code execution on the host machine.

### Security Guarantees
This architecture prevents dependencies from claiming false security properties. It stops hidden code execution during package resolution. It ensures that incorrect manifests cannot increase the capabilities of a module. However, the architecture has limitations. It does not prevent correctly signed packages from performing malicious actions within their approved capabilities. To mitigate this remaining risk, the design requires strict mathematical contracts. It also requires human reviews for highly privileged components.

## Conclusion
The verifiable web of code provides a secure foundation for package management. It supports the openOODA ecosystem with a zero-trust model. The system integrates capability manifests and mathematical proofs directly into the package structure. This integration eliminates common vulnerabilities in the software supply chain. The architecture ensures that every imported module respects the capability limits of the system. This design allows artificial intelligence agents and human developers to safely compose complex software from decentralized sources.

## References
1. G. Necula and P. Lee, "Proof-Carrying Code," Proceedings of the 24th ACM SIGPLAN-SIGACT Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages (POPL), 1997.
2. S. Torres-Arias et al., "in-toto: Securing the Software Supply Chain," USENIX Security Symposium, 2019.
3. The Update Framework (TUF) specification.
4. M. Miller, "Robust Composition: Towards a Unified Approach to Access Control and Concurrency Control," PhD thesis, Johns Hopkins University, 2006.

